Tracing the resolved behaviour: latestAnswer()/latestTimestamp()/latestRound() do indeed skip _checkSequencer() and
  call _calculateBaseToQuote() (PendlePTOracle override: Chainlink latestRoundData() on the base feed with
  require(baseToUSD > 0), then the Pendle TWAP _getPTRate()), so the factual observation in the claim is accurate. But
  it is only an observation about an unused interface surface, not an exploit. All of these are view functions that
  mutate nothing; the only in-protocol consumer of this oracle is the TradingModule, which reads latestRoundData() — the
  path that does call _checkSequencer(). The sibling AggregatorV2 legacy methods in this same file (getRoundData,
  getAnswer, getTimestamp) are hard-revert()ed and explicitly annotated "Unused in the trading module", which is direct
  evidence that the V2 half of the interface is legacy surface the protocol does not route through; latestAnswer() is
  left non-reverting only for interface compatibility. The claim's exploit is stated purely hypothetically ("any
  consumer ... wired to the AggregatorV2 half"), and no such consumer exists in the code shown or in the protocol's own
  integration path. An unprivileged attacker calling latestAnswer() during a sequencer outage gets a number back and can
  do nothing with it inside this system, because every state-changing protocol path that prices these assets goes
  through latestRoundData() and reverts with "Sequencer Down"/"Sequencer Grace Period". Harm would require a third-party
  integrator that the protocol does not control and that is not part of this codebase — i.e. the exact step that breaks
  a protocol invariant cannot be pointed at. Under the tie-break rule, unproven-consumer speculation is insufficient.
  VERDICT|REFUTED|src/oracles/AbstractCustomOracle.sol:latestAnswer:64|C2|latestAnswer() is an unused legacy
  AggregatorV2 view (its siblings getRoundData/getAnswer/getTimestamp hard-revert and are annotated "Unused in the
  trading module"), while every actual protocol consumer prices through latestRoundData(), which does call
  _checkSequencer() — so no unprivileged state-changing path bypasses the sequencer gate.
